A||||4||pontiacs4me||how much power can you get from a 3.4?||||1||
Z||000000||pontiacs4me||03-26-2001||03:50 AM||||Ive been looking around in my local newspaper lately for a 1986 buick centry, or something of the sort.  My mom used to have one, that car hauled!!!  I wasted a friend of mine with a 1990 rs camaro 305 with a 5 spd.  I mean within 5 seconds off the line I was 3 cars in front of him.  I dont remember what engine that car had, I think it was the biggest v6 available for it at the time.  Im guessing it was a 3.4 liter.  Now for some questions.  Are the front wheel drive transaxles the same as the fieros?  If so I was planning to pick up a good running car, take out the engine and transaxle, along with the ecm and all the goods, rebuild everything, and put it in my fiero.  If I do some mods, say bigger cam, ported heads and intake, maybe some custom headers, bigger injectors, stuff like that, how much hp and torque should I be looking at?  Im really wanting to find a 3800 supercharged from a gtp or something, but that swap is pretty expensive in the long run with all the conversion parts etc. etc.  I would like to have 250 hp, is this possible with a 3.4 liter?  I dont have much experience with 6 cylinder engines.  I have built a few v8's which is no biggy to get 400 horse out of, but these little V6's I have no Idea.  Also, If I get an engine out of a 87 buick, wont the ecm's be practically identical to my 87 fiero?  It will be practically just a bolt in deal wont it?  Also, does anybody make a supercharger for these small v6's?  as much info as possible would be greatly appreciated. thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000001||pontiacs4me||03-26-2001||04:13 AM||||Ok,<BR>I just found out something, the engine was most likely a 3.8 liter, 231 v6.  I didnt know 3.8 liters existed way back then.  (Im new to the v6 game)  so how hard would this swap be?  wont the wiring and things be pretty compatable?  Id rather do this than the supercharged 3.8 much more cost effective, and I can spend more internally to make the same power.  what kind of power did these engines make in 1986?  What transmission do you guys recommend so Im not blowing them up every week.  It seemed my mom went through about 4 used trannys in the 6 years she had the car.  I told her she should buy a rebuilt one but she insisted on finding a junk yard tranny that would hold up.  but they never did.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Tryxalon||03-26-2001||08:18 AM||||The Buick-made  3.8 liter (3800, 231 cubic inch) v-6 is a 90 degree V-6 based on a V-6 design that has been around since about 1968 or so. <P>In 1976, Smokey Yunick (motor builder and race car driver) Built one that got 290 horsepower at the flywheel with a single 4bbl carb. <P>By mid 80's it was a nice engine somewhere around 160 to 180 HP in fuel injected form and propelling the post 86 "full sized" cars nicely with 20 MPG, but extremely durable. <P> Series II came along in the late 80's/early 90's with several improvements and HP increased to about 195 or so.  1995 this was improved with the current form getting 205 hp<P>Supercharged versions get more, and the Buick Grand National Regals were dual turbocharged and got trememdous amounts of power. <P>You will need a special flywheel and some other modifications but there are many, many of the engine out there.  Do a search and you can find several swaps.<P> <A HREF="http://www.terranexus.com/fieroman/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.terranexus.com/fieroman/</A>   <BR>is only one site describing such a swap. <P><p>[This message has been edited by Tryxalon (edited 03-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||lou_dias||03-26-2001||09:49 AM||||Actually the GN's and Regals had a single turbo and the GNX has a big intercooler and a little more boost.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Formula88||03-26-2001||09:54 AM||||Also, the GN engine was the 3.8 Litre, not the 3800.  Different generations of the same engine.  I guess that would make it a Series I?  Yes, the GN had only 1 HUGE turbo.  Same as the GNX, but the GNX had a ceramic turbine for less turbo lag.  I had an '87 GN back then (my first new car).  Man did that thing haul!  It was also the only car I've seen chips make a differnece in.  I used the Kenne-Bell hi-lo Pro chip and it bumped the boost from 14psi to 19!  All that with just a chip change (and thermostat).  Still, my Formula's more fun to drive.  I'd be hard pressed to choose between my Formula 5-speed and the '72 455HO Trans Am automatic I had back in college as to which is more fun... I think the Fiero's more fun.  It definately handles better, but the TA wins the horsepower wars hands down.  It was even faster than the GN, bone stock!||||reg||1
